industrial society is what's what's here  to stay  meanwhile the bourgeoisie enlists the  proletariat in its own class struggles  so this is going to be very important  she says when the workers actually get  together to form bodies it's not the  consequence of their own active union  but the union of the bourgeoisie  which is trying to attain its own  political ends and it enlists the  workers it enlists the proletariat on  its side so the proletarians did not  fight their enemies he says but the  enemies of their enemies the remnants of  absolute monarchy the landowners the non  industrial bourgeoisie the petty  bourgeoisie they're they're all enemies  of the bourgeoisie who wants to  radicalize everything but then you know  keep things at that point and the  proletariat doesn't really realize that  they're they're not helping themselves  out by doing this they're actually May  things worse it's sort of like if you  were working at one of these big-box  stores and the the owners and the  management say we're under attack from  the local government we need all of you  to go in and protest outside City Hall  it might not be the end of the workers  interest to do that as a matter of fact  the workers might be getting screwed  maybe the best thing for the workers to  do would be to line up with City Hall  against their management and ownership  but they don't do that at first but in  the process of engaging in this fight  they begin to acquire some of the  intellectual tools they begin to acquire  some of the consciousness of their own  agency of their own ability to do  something and the next step is actual  development of their own unions they  begin to say forget it I don't need the  bourgeoisie let's get together ourselves  so he says with the development of  Industry the proletariat increases a  number but it becomes concentrated in  greater masses if strength grows and it  feels that strength it's becoming  conscious of its strength so growing  competition among the bourgeoisie brings  about commercial crises and the workers  then begin to say we need to do  something more rational the management  the owners they don't care about us when  things all go to hell they're fine and  they're their house on the hill we're  the ones who are suffering let's get  together and make sure that things  actually go the way that they're  supposed to go and so we see the  development of trade unions and then  fairly soon after that organization into  a political party or political parties  they're engaging in the political  process now and trying to have a say in  in their own destiny and how things go  now as this goes on there's going to be  some what we can call defect ations  from the bourgeoisie some of these are  involuntary these these processes of  industrial transformation are going to  take some of the people from the  bourgeoisie and turn them into losers  and the great game  and they were gonna fall from their high  status and say owners or manufacturers  down into the bourgeoisie same thing is  going to happen with some of the other  classes as well more and more people are  going to get added to it now those  people tend to be fairly well educated  and they also tend to have habits of  regarding themselves as possessing a  certain degree of dignity or agency and  they contribute they they when they  become part of the proletariat when they  become poor they can do an organizing  role and this actually has a very  important part in revolutionary  struggles he also talks about a small  section of the ruling class cuts itself  adrift and joins the revolutionary class  why do they do that  because they see that the revolutionary  class has as Marx and Engels who they're  saying the future in its hands they want  to line themselves up with where history  is going so some of the bourgeoisie  leave just just as much as in the French  Revolution one of the ways that that was  able to happen some of the aristocracy  joined the side of the bourgeoisie the  French Revolution was a bourgeois  rebellion by the way or a revolution now  where do we where do we go to he talks  about the the awareness and here's where  the class consciousness is ultimately  going the awareness of the illegitimate  see of the bourgeoisie the fact that the  bourgeoisie is as he says not fit to run  society why well because it's it's  generated a lot of wealth it's it's  actually created vast opportunities but  it's also made the condition for many  people worse and if we're going to say  who should run society how should  society structure which is a political  question as well as an economic question  the answer has got to be those who are  going to take care of us those who are  going to make conditions better the  bourgeoisie has shown that they can't  provide that according to Marx so he  says all other historical movement  we're movements of minorities this is  actually the self-conscious independent  movement of the immense majority in the  interest of the immense majority the  proletariat cannot raise itself cannot  raise itself up without the whole strand  of official society being knocked aside  being sprang into the air so what do we  need in this case and this is the the  Marxist response to this this is where  if you've been following along so far  you might say here's where I differ  because a lot of socialists actually  yeah is revolution that's the only thing  that is going to change these conditions  that's going to elevate mankind out of  this dog-eat-dog existence that the  bourgeois has led people into so he says  the bourgeois is unfit to rule because  it's incompetent to assure an existence  to its slave within his slavery it  cannot help letting him sink into such a  state it has to feed him instead of  being fed by him Society can no longer  live under this bourgeoisie in other  words its existence is no longer  compatible with society another thing  that you can say here is sort of society  humanity Marx and Engels think that  there's a lot to be said on the part of  the bourgeoisie for what they've done  they've opened up people's eyes they've  swept away to the previous inequalities  and you know traditional modes of  imposing power on people they manage to  create vast wealth but they haven't  delivered on what they want to do and so  now it's time for them to get out of the  way if they won't get out of the way  they have to be swept out of the way
 

